\section{Introduction}
Foau (ISO 639-3 flh) is a Lakes Plain language of Papua, Indonesia. The search results indicate it is now usually called Abawiri. Only academic description was found. No school materials, primers or community resources appeared.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
None found.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Brendon Yoder, \emph{A grammar of Abawiri} (UC Santa Barbara dissertation, 2020), the first full description of the language, per its abstract: \url{https://escholarship.org/uc/item/4mj571tr}
\item Glottolog entry for Foau: \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/languoid/id/foau1240}
\item Wikipedia article on Abawiri (secondary summary of phonology): \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abawiri}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
None found.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
The language is described as having seven vowels, complex tone and no nasal consonants. Before Yoder's work it was known mostly from short wordlists. Any curriculum would need an orthography and materials built with the community.
\end{document}
